 "equipment_type": "Power and energy meter (BACnet MS/TP)",
 "applies_to": "WattNode BACnet meters WNC-3Y-xxx-BN and WNC-3D-xxx-BN, plus the revenue-grade RWNC-...-BN models, firmware 1.13 and 1.20.",
 "summary": "The WattNode BACnet is a three-phase power and energy meter with BACnet MS/TP on board. It has a fixed object list: Analog Inputs for energy, power, voltage, current, frequency, power factor and demand; Analog Values for error history, diagnostics and setup (CT amps, demand period, PT ratio, gain and phase trims); Binary Values for CT polarity and reset commands; and Multi-state Values for service type, averaging and demand reset.",
 "bacnet_transport": [
  "BACnet MS/TP"
 ],
 "firmware": "1.13 and 1.20 (manual WNC-BN-1.20)",
 "default_device_instance": "Meter serial number",
 "access_inferred": false,
 "notes": [
  "Objects the manual marks as kept across power failures include the energy totals, error history, CT and demand setup, PhaseOffset and Averaging; energy is saved to non-volatile flash.",
  "AI 58 ThermistorTemp and AV 35-37 thermistor coefficients exist only on meters with Option TM1 (firmware 1.20).",
  "Energy objects AI 38-57 and the demand objects can be zeroed or preset by writing them; AI 0-2 (NR) can never be reset.",
  "Access: W marks objects the manual's PICS lists as writable, the presettable energy objects (AI 36-57) and the BV/MV commands. Demand (AI 31-35), ErrorStatus (AV 0-7) and the RS-485 counters are R: they are reset only through ZeroDemand (MV 2) or ClearErrors (BV 3). Access cross-checked with Continental's object spreadsheet, https://ctlsys.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/WattNode-BACnet-Objects-V120.xls.",
  "The manual's table prints AI 42 as 'EnerygB' (typo); the spreadsheet names it EnergyB. AI 31 is 'DemandSum' in the manual and 'Demand' in the spreadsheet.",
  "The manual lists DemandApp (AI 32) in W although it is the apparent power demand."
 ],
 "source": {
  "title": "WattNode BACnet Electric Power Meter - Reference Manual",
  "doc_number": "WNC-BN-1.20",
  "revision_date": "2017-08-25",
  "url": "https://ctlsys.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/09/WNC-BACnet-Manual-V1_20.pdf"
 },
 "integration": {
  "summary": "Set the MS/TP address and baud rate on the DIP switch, then set CtAmpsA/B/C (AV 12-14) to the rated amps of the CTs and pick the service type (MV 0). After that, read total power (AI 3), net energy (AI 0 or the resettable AI 38), demand (AI 31) and the phase voltages and currents. ErrorStatus1 (AV 0) holds the newest error code.",
  "minimum_points": [
   {
    "ref": "AI 3",
    "name": "PowerSum",
    "why": "Total real power, W"
   },
   {
    "ref": "AI 0",
    "name": "EnergySumNR",
    "why": "Net energy total, kWh, never resets"
   },
   {
    "ref": "AI 31",
    "name": "DemandSum",
    "why": "Real power demand, W"
   },
   {
    "ref": "AI 15",
    "name": "VoltAvgLN",
    "why": "Average line-to-neutral voltage"
   },
   {
    "ref": "AI 24",
    "name": "CurrentA",
    "why": "Phase A current, A"
   },
   {
    "ref": "AI 27",
    "name": "PowerFactorAvg",
    "why": "Average power factor"
   },
   {
    "ref": "AI 23",
    "name": "Freq",
    "why": "Line frequency, Hz"
   },
   {
    "ref": "AV 0",
    "name": "ErrorStatus1",
    "why": "Newest error code, 0 = no errors"
   }
  ],
  "enable": null,
  "heartbeat": null,
  "remote_sensors": null,
  "setup": [
   "MS/TP MAC address 0-63 set with DIP switches 1-6 (factory default 0).",
   "Baud rate on DIP switches 7 and 8: 9600 (default), 19200, 38400 or 76800.",
   "The device instance defaults to the meter serial number and can be changed over the network; the change survives power loss.",
   "After a DIP switch change the meter drops offline until the switches have been stable for five seconds.",
   "Set CtAmpsA/B/C (AV 12-14), and PtRatio (AV 24) if potential transformers are used; wrong values make every reading wrong."
  ],
  "gotchas": [
   "Writing ZeroEnergy (BV 4) zeroes all resettable energy objects; the NR totals (AI 0-2) keep counting.",
   "Changing the demand setup (AV 15-16) or the CT setup (CtAmps, InvertCt) zeroes the demand readings.",
   "A ConfigPasscode (AV 11) locks configuration writes until the passcode is written again.",
   "Calibrate (AV 25) is a factory object; do not write it."
  ]
 },
